  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "Solve $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}-2\\ge 1.$\n\n$x\\in ($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$]\u22c3[$\n{{field-3}}$,$\n{{field-4}}$)$",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's simplify the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}-2 & \\ge 1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{6} & \\ge 1+2 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{6} & \\ge 3 \\\\ {x}^{2} & \\ge 18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use either of two methods to solve the inequality.\n\n*Method 1 - Solving an Absolute Value Equation*\n\nTaking the square root of both sides of the inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & \\ge \\sqrt{18} \\\\ |x| & \\ge 3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the definition of absolute value, we must have \n\n$$\nx\\le -3\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{or}\\,x\\ge 3\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, the solution set is $x\\in (-\\infty ,-3\\sqrt{2}]\\cup [3\\sqrt{2},\\infty ).$\n\n*Method 2 - Picturing a Quadratic Curve*\n\nStarting from ${x}^{2}\\ge 18$ and moving all terms to the left-hand side, we reach\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-18\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding parabola given by $\\,y={x}^{2}-18\\,$ and find its roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-18 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{18})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-{(3\\sqrt{2})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (x-3\\sqrt{2})(x+3\\sqrt{2}) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nhence $x=\\pm 3\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nSince the leading coefficient of ${x}^{2}-18$   is positive, the parabola  opens upward.  Therefore, the solution set to the  given inequality ($y\\ge 0$) lies outside the roots (and  includes them):\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13965374/assets/72488926dd64954e2c1070a94625ebc91c1c729d70bbf7c5f098364e651efec5.png)\n\nTherefore, the solution is \n\n$$\nx\\in (-\\infty ,-3\\sqrt{2}]\\cup [3\\sqrt{2},\\infty ).\n$$",
